{"kind":"request","text":"codex: design how a person plays chess from the browser. Hugh asked for this; I have read the current surface and the gap is narrower and more interesting than it looks, so this asks for a design rather than an implementation.\n\nWHAT ALREADY EXISTS, which surprised me. The browser is already a signing party. app.js generates an Ed25519 keypair with window.crypto.subtle.generateKey({name: \"Ed25519\"}, false, [\"sign\",\"verify\"]), sends the public key to /v1/live/session/prepare, signs the returned challenge and opens a session. It signs chat submissions the same way. So challenge-response, fingerprints and per-game session actors are all built and working.\n\nWHAT IS MISSING, precisely. Two things, and only two.\n\nFirst, the browser key is ephemeral. It is generated fresh in a module-scoped variable each time a session opens, and generateKey is called with extractable false, so nothing persists and nothing can be exported. That is exactly right for a leased presence identity and exactly wrong for a seat: a durable seat binds to an actor fingerprint, and this fingerprint dies on reload.\n\nSecond, the web surface never writes durably. runServe builds newReadHandler; there is no openWriter, no host append, no signing of a durable intent anywhere in web.go. Every POST under /v1/live is presence, chat or motion, and the code says so plainly — motion hints live only in leased presence and disappear on renewal, revocation or expiry. So the UI can show the board, list legal moves, and show the opponent your drag in flight, but the move is committed by chess move on the command line. That is the whole of the gap.\n\nTHE SHAPE I THINK IS RIGHT, offered as a starting point and not as an instruction. The host package says any syntactically valid act carrying a good signature over a well-formed intent is admitted, and that the submit path is open to whoever holds a key. The browser holds a key. So the natural design is that the browser key becomes a real chess actor: it joins a seat durably, signs its own move intents locally, and the server relays the signed act without ever holding a player private key. A non-extractable CryptoKey can be persisted in IndexedDB, so the key survives reloads while the private half never leaves the browser and never reaches the server. The server gains no new authority, which means the loopback restriction keeps meaning what it means today.\n\nWHAT MAKES ME WANT A DESIGN FIRST. That shape moves a trust boundary. Today the serve process cannot forge a move because it holds no key; whatever is built must not quietly give it one. And browser-side intent encoding is the kind of duplication that goes wrong silently — two encoders that agree in testing and diverge on an edge case produce a bad signature in a permanent log. Both deserve deciding before either is written.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT ASKING FOR. Not a change to the fold, not a relaxation of the loopback or trusted-host rules, and not a stopgap where the browser posts a move for the CLI to sign later. If the answer is that a server-held key is genuinely the better design, say so and argue it — I would rather be shown wrong than have my sketch implemented because I filed it.","body":{"conditions":"Produce a design, not an implementation. It must answer, with reference to the code as it stands: (1) Identity. Whether the browser's Ed25519 key becomes a durable chess actor that holds a seat in its own right, or whether some delegation to a server-held key is proposed instead. State which and why. If the browser key is the actor, say how it persists across reloads while staying non-extractable, and what happens when it is lost — a seat bound to a key nobody holds is a game nobody can finish. (2) The submit path. How a move signed in the browser reaches the log: what the server does with it, and the explicit statement of whether the serve process ever holds or uses a player private key. If the answer is that it does not, say what stops a hostile page from having the server sign for it, since that is the property the current read-only surface gets for free and would be spending. (3) Intent encoding in the browser. A durable act needs the exact signed bytes; the browser today signs only live challenge bytes supplied by the server. Say how the move intent is encoded and who is responsible for its correctness, and how a mismatch between browser and server encodings is caught by a test rather than by a bad signature in the log. (4) Seat binding. How joining a seat from the UI works, given SeatFor keys seats by actor fingerprint, and whether an existing CLI-joined seat can be played from a browser or only a browser-joined one. (5) Scope boundary. What stays out: at minimum this design does not change the loopback restriction, does not weaken TrustedHostHandler, and does not alter the fold. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. The security section must treat the browser as untrusted input even though it is same-origin. No code changes in this lane; the design lands as a note with an exact-head artifact and independent review, and implementation becomes its own request after hugh ratifies the design.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}